  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 119 AM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAnother hot day is on tap for the forecast area today, as an\nanomalous upper-level ridge is centered over the Desert Southwest.\nThere is high confidence that temperatures will return to the \nupper 90s and low 100s across the majority of the forecast area as\n700 mb temperatures are expected to reach 20-24C by the \nafternoon. With afternoon high temps forecast to range from 12-17\ndegrees above normal across the urban corridor and Weld County, a\nHeat Advisory remains in effect beginning at 1200 PM today for \nthese locations, and will continue through Monday afternoon, as \ntemperatures are forecast to heat into the upper 90s and low 100s \nonce again Monday. It should be noted that an approaching cold \nfront associated with a passing shortwave to our north may reach \nthe forecast area before peak heating occurs Monday afternoon. \nLatest guidance hints at a late afternoon arrival time, and with \nflow aloft turning more zonal as the shortwave flattens out the \nridge, downsloping winds and the poor overnight recoveries \nexpected tonight have kept confidence high enough that the current\nHeat Advisory should remain as is, as temps are going to start \nout very warm in the morning and heat up quickly before the front \nmakes it here, making for another very hot day along the majority \nof the urban corridor, with heat relief expected from north to \nsouth behind the front. If the front arrives sooner than currently\nprogged, we could see recoveries slightly sooner, therefore we \nwill continue to monitor latest trends and adjust the Heat \nAdvisory if need be. \n\nOutside of the heat, a tightening pressure gradient is expected to\nincrease surface winds across the eastern plains later today.\nRelative humidities (RH) are expected to range from 9% to the mid\nteens across the lower elevations, but winds are expected to be \nstrongest where RH values are highest, and while there may be \nsome localized critical fire weather conditions at times across \nthe far eastern plains, we are not expecting them to reach the \nthree hour time threshold at any given location to warrant any \nfire weather highlights. With the uncertainties on the timing of\nthe front on Monday, there could be some overlapping low RH with\ngusty winds across Weld County in the afternoon that will bring\nelevated to near-critical fire weather conditions until we see\nsome RH recovery behind the front. \n\nPrecipitation chances aren't expected to return until Monday\nwhere scattered afternoon showers will be possible along the\nhigher elevations and Palmer Divide before some storms will be\npossible with the arrival of the cold front in the afternoon and\nevening, but any accumulations are not expected to be much more\nthan a few hundredths. \n\nWhile we are expecting a few days of heat relief behind the cold\nfront, increasing winds aloft will start to introduce potential\nfor elevated to critical fire weather conditions across the higher\nelevations where RH values are expected to drop into the single\ndigits by midweek. The cooler weather isn't expected to last long,\nand afternoon highs are expected to return to the mid to upper 90s\nacross the plains by Thursday afternoon as the ridge rebuilds over\nthe western CONUS, and temperatures gradually warm each day into\nthe weekend. Precipitation chances will remain limited through the\nextended forecast period.",
